# Solving single pendulums by DifferentialEquations.jl

In this notebook, we shall solve the single pendulum equation:

```math
\ddot q = -\sin q,
```
where $q$ means the angle.

Hamiltonian:

```math
H(q,p) = \frac{1}{2}p^2 - \cos q + 1.
```
Canonical equation:

```math
\dot q = p, \quad \dot p = - \sin q.
```
Initial condition:

```math
q(0) = 0, \quad p(0) = 2k.
```
Exact solution:

```math
q(t) = 2\arcsin(k\,\mathrm{sn}(t,k)).
```
Maximum of $q(t)$:

```math
\sin(q_{\max}/2) = k, \quad q_{\max} = \max\{q(t)\}.
```
Define $y(t)$ by

```math
y(t) = \sin(q(t)/2) = k\,\mathrm{sn}(t,k), \quad y_{\max} = k.
```

For each run, the "Energy" panel reports the standard deviation of $H$ along the trajectory,
and the "Comparison" panel overlays the numerical $y(t)$ on the exact $k\,\mathrm{sn}(t - t_0, k)$,
where $t_0$ is the start of the integration, and reports the maximum absolute difference.
```julia
# Single pendulums shall be solved numerically.
#
using OrdinaryDiffEq, Elliptic, Printf, DiffEqPhysics, Statistics
using OrdinaryDiffEqSymplecticRK

# `HamiltonianProblem(H, p0, q0, tspan)` stores the state as `ArrayPartition(p, q)`:
# the first half of `u` is the momentum and the second half is the position.
sol2p(sol) = [sol.u[i][j] for i in 1:length(sol.u), j in 1:(length(sol.u[1]) ÷ 2)]
function sol2q(sol)
    [sol.u[i][j] for i in 1:length(sol.u), j in (length(sol.u[1]) ÷ 2 + 1):length(sol.u[1])]
end
sol2tqp(sol) = (sol.t, sol2q(sol), sol2p(sol))

# The exact solutions of single pendulums can be expressed by the Jacobian elliptic functions.
#
sn(u, k) = Jacobi.sn(u, k^2) # the Jacobian sn function

# Use Plot.
#
using Plots

# Define the color list
colorlist = [
    "#1f77b4", "#ff7f0e", "#2ca02c", "#d62728", "#9467bd",
    "#8c564b", "#e377c2", "#7f7f7f", "#bcbd22", "#17becf"
]
cc(k) = colorlist[mod1(k, length(colorlist))]

# Function to plot the solution of a Hamiltonian problem
function plotsol(sol::ODESolution)
    local t, q, p
    t, q, p = sol2tqp(sol)
    local d = size(q)[2]
    p1 = plot(title = "Solution", xlabel = "t", grid = :on)
    for j in 1:d
        j_str = d > 1 ? "[$j]" : ""
        plot!(p1, t, q[:, j], color = cc(2j-1), label = "q$(j_str)", linewidth = 1)
        plot!(p1, t, p[:, j], color = cc(2j), label = "p$(j_str)",
            linewidth = 1, linestyle = :dash)
    end
    return p1
end

# Function to plot the solution on the 2D phase space
function plotsol2(sol::ODESolution)
    local t, q, p
    t, q, p = sol2tqp(sol)
    local d = size(q)[2]
    p2 = plot(title = "Phase Space", xlabel = "q", ylabel = "p", grid = :on)
    for j in 1:d
        j_str = d > 1 ? "[$j]" : ""
        plot!(p2, q[:, j], p[:, j], color = cc(j),
            label = "(q$(j_str), p$(j_str))", linewidth = 1)
    end
    return p2
end

# Function to plot the energy of a Hamiltonian problem
function plotenergy(H, sol::ODESolution)
    local t, q, p
    t, q, p = sol2tqp(sol)
    local energy = [H(p[i, :], q[i, :], nothing) for i in 1:size(q)[1]]
    p3 = plot(t, energy, label = "energy", color = "red", linewidth = 1,
        xlabel = "t", title = "Energy", grid = :on)
    stdenergy_str = @sprintf("%.3e", std(energy))
    title!("std(energy) = $stdenergy_str", fontsize = 10)
    return p3
end

# Function to compare the numerical and exact solutions of a single pendulum
function plotcomparison(k, sol::ODESolution)
    local t, q, p
    t, q, p = sol2tqp(sol)
    local y = sin.(q / 2)
    local y_exact = k * sn.(t .- t[1], k) # exact solution with q(t[1]) = 0, p(t[1]) = 2k

    p4 = plot(t, y, label = "numerical", linewidth = 1, grid = :on,
        xlabel = "t", ylabel = "y = sin(q(t)/2)", title = "Comparison")
    plot!(p4, t, y_exact, label = "exact", linewidth = 1, linestyle = :dash)
    error_str = @sprintf("%.3e", maximum(abs.(y - y_exact)))
    title!("max|y - y_exact| = $error_str", fontsize = 10)
    return p4
end

# Plot solution and energy
function plotsolenergy(H, integrator, Δt, sol::ODESolution)
    integrator_str = replace("$integrator", r"^[^.]*\." => "")

    p1 = plotsol(sol)
    p2 = plotsol2(sol)
    p3 = plotenergy(H, sol)

    suptitle = "===== $integrator_str, Δt = $Δt ====="
    plot(p1, p2, p3, layout = (3, 1), plot_title = suptitle, size = (900, 700))
end

# Solve a single pendulum
function singlependulum(k, integrator, Δt; t0 = 0.0, t1 = 100.0)
    H(p, q, params) = p[1]^2 / 2 - cos(q[1]) + 1
    q0 = [0.0]
    p0 = [2k]
    prob = HamiltonianProblem(H, p0, q0, (t0, t1))

    integrator_str = replace("$integrator", r"^[^.]*\." => "")
    @printf("%-25s", "$integrator_str:")
    sol = solve(prob, integrator, dt = Δt)
    @time sol = solve(prob, integrator, dt = Δt)

    sleep(0.1)

    t, q, p = sol2tqp(sol)
    energy = [H(p[i, :], q[i, :], nothing) for i in 1:length(t)]
    y_error = maximum(abs.(sin.(q[:, 1] / 2) .- k * sn.(t .- t[1], k)))
    @printf("%-25s std(energy) = %.3e, max|y - y_exact| = %.3e\n", "", std(energy), y_error)

    p1 = plotsol(sol)
    p2 = plotsol2(sol)
    p3 = plotenergy(H, sol)
    p4 = plotcomparison(k, sol)

    suptitle = "===== $integrator_str, Δt = $Δt ====="
    plot(p1, p2, p3, p4, layout = (2, 2), plot_title = suptitle, size = (900, 700))
end
```

## Tests

```julia
# Single pendulum
k = rand()
integrator = VelocityVerlet()
Δt = 0.1
singlependulum(k, integrator, Δt, t0 = -20.0, t1 = 20.0)
```

```julia
# Two single pendulums
H(p, q, param) = sum(p .^ 2 / 2 .- cos.(q) .+ 1)
q0 = pi * rand(2)
p0 = zeros(2)
t0, t1 = -20.0, 20.0
prob = HamiltonianProblem(H, p0, q0, (t0, t1))

integrator = McAte4()
Δt = 0.1
sol = solve(prob, integrator, dt = Δt)
@time sol = solve(prob, integrator, dt = Δt)

sleep(0.1)
plotsolenergy(H, integrator, Δt, sol)
```

## Comparison of symplectic integrators

```julia
SymplecticIntegrators = [
    SymplecticEuler(),
    VelocityVerlet(),
    VerletLeapfrog(),
    PseudoVerletLeapfrog(),
    McAte2(),
    Ruth3(),
    McAte3(),
    CandyRoz4(),
    McAte4(),
    CalvoSanz4(),
    McAte42(),
    McAte5(),
    Yoshida6(),
    KahanLi6(),
    McAte8(),
    KahanLi8(),
    SofSpa10()
]

k = 0.999
Δt = 0.1
for integrator in SymplecticIntegrators
    display(singlependulum(k, integrator, Δt))
end
```

```julia
k = 0.999
Δt = 0.01
for integrator in SymplecticIntegrators[1:4]
    display(singlependulum(k, integrator, Δt))
end
```

```julia
k = 0.999
Δt = 0.001
singlependulum(k, SymplecticEuler(), Δt)
```

```julia
k = 0.999
Δt = 0.0001
singlependulum(k, SymplecticEuler(), Δt)
```

## Results

With $k = 0.999$ the pendulum starts close to the separatrix, and over $t \in [0, 100]$ with
$\Delta t = 0.1$ the second-order methods (`SymplecticEuler`, `VelocityVerlet`, `VerletLeapfrog`,
`PseudoVerletLeapfrog`, `McAte2`) accumulate enough phase error that the maximum difference to the
exact $y(t)$ is of order 1, even though their energy error stays bounded (standard deviation between
about 1e-4 and 3e-2). The third- to fifth-order methods reach phase errors between about 1e-4 and
4e-2, the sixth-order `Yoshida6` and `KahanLi6` reach 1e-5 to 1e-6, and the eighth- and tenth-order
`McAte8`, `KahanLi8` and `SofSpa10` reach 1e-9 to 1e-11 with energy fluctuations near machine precision.
`McAte42` is the exception among the fourth-order methods: it only achieves second order
(a known bug, [OrdinaryDiffEq.jl#85](https://github.com/SciML/OrdinaryDiffEq.jl/issues/85)),
so its energy standard deviation of about 6e-3 and phase error of order 1 match the
second-order methods. With $\Delta t = 0.01$ the
second-order methods reach phase errors of about 5e-2 to 9e-2, and `SymplecticEuler` reaches about 1e-3
at $\Delta t = 0.001$ and 1e-5 at $\Delta t = 0.0001$.

